  "question": {
    "text": "The figure below shows an isosceles triangular frame <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>ABC</mi></math>&nbsp;made out of two different materials and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></math>&nbsp;is the midpoint of the side <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>AB</mi></math>. The coefficient of thermal expansion of the rod <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>ADB</mi></math> is <math><msub><mrow><mi>&#945;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> and for the rods&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>AC</mi></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>BC</mi></math> is <math><msub><mrow><mi>&#945;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>.</mo></math> End <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> is fixed and the whole system is placed on a smooth horizontal surface. When&nbsp;the temperature of the system increases, it is found that the distance remains&nbsp;fixed, then<br />\r<br />\r<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/nta_abhyas/jee_main/images/tpznuihytttlgnbhjbeccqbmqroagfvhuljefreagvoyhtihpo.png\" />",
    "image": null
  },
